is performed. Look,

271
00:14:39,519 --> 00:14:46,440
listen carefully to this part so that you understand

272
00:14:42,800 --> 00:14:49,839
the subject completely. We have

273
00:14:46,440 --> 00:14:53,680
defined a function here called button_debounce,

274
00:14:49,839 --> 00:14:56,120
and this function is

275
00:14:53,680 --> 00:14:58,959
defined as an 8-bit function.

276
00:14:56,120 --> 00:15:01,720
and it has two inputs. These two inputs

277
00:14:58,959 --> 00:15:03,160
are what? One is the port, the port that

278
00:15:01,720 --> 00:15:05,720
we want to define. Here we defined GPIOA,

279
00:15:03,160 --> 00:15:08,560
and the other is the pin we are interested in.

280
00:15:05,720 --> 00:15:12,800
So it has two inputs: the port

281
00:15:08,560 --> 00:15:16,880
and the pin of that port.

282
00:15:12,800 --> 00:15:20,360
When this function is called, it enters

283
00:15:16,880 --> 00:15:24,680
this line. What does this line say?

284
00:15:20,360 --> 00:15:27,040
Look, in the HAL library we have a function

285
00:15:24,680 --> 00:15:30,560
called HAL_GPIO_ReadPin. What does ReadPin do?

286
00:15:27,040 --> 00:15:34,880
It reads the value

287
00:15:30,560 --> 00:15:37,240
of the port and pin where an event is happening.

288
00:15:34,880 --> 00:15:39,639
For example, on port A, pin 0, an event happens:

289
00:15:37,240 --> 00:15:42,519
it becomes 0 or 1, and whatever

290
00:15:39,639 --> 00:15:47,199
value it has is read by this function.

291
00:15:42,519 --> 00:15:49,959
That value is then stored in reading.

292
00:15:47,199 --> 00:15:53,880
Now we said that, for example, in the initial state,

293
00:15:49,959 --> 00:15:57,040
we said that PA0, as I showed you here,

294
00:15:53,880 --> 00:16:00,839
is initially

295
00:15:57,040 --> 00:16:04,800
configured as pull-up.

296
00:16:00,839 --> 00:16:07,920
That means

297
00:16:04,800 --> 00:16:11,839
the initial value of GPIOA pin 0

298
00:16:07,920 --> 00:16:16,680
is one, so the initial reading value

299
00:16:11,839 --> 00:16:21,399
is one. Now we say that if this

300
00:16:16,680 --> 00:16:23,800
reading value, which is one with pull-up, is different from last

301
00:16:21,399 --> 00:16:26,600
button state, what does that mean? Last button

302
00:16:23,800 --> 00:16:28,759
state initially has the value one, or SET. It says if

303
00:16:26,600 --> 00:16:31,720
it is different from reading. It is not, so it does not enter

304
00:16:28,759 --> 00:16:35,800
this loop and it does not enter the next loop either.

305
00:16:31,720 --> 00:16:38,560
It comes down here and says

306
00:16:35,800 --> 00:16:41,240
last_button_state should be equal to reading. Since

307
00:16:38,560 --> 00:16:43,399
reading is one and last_button_state is one, it

308
00:16:41,240 --> 00:16:47,000
returns FALSE. What does return FALSE mean?

309
00:16:43,399 --> 00:16:49,440
It goes back to the while loop and says

310
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:52,040
do not return to the main action. If return

311
00:16:49,440 --> 00:16:54,000
is zero, it does not return to the while loop to

312
00:16:52,040 --> 00:16:56,440
for example, increase a number or do anything else.

313
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:58,800
The meaning of return zero is

314
00:16:56,440 --> 00:17:00,560
that it does not return to the main loop; the function

315
00:16:58,800 --> 00:17:03,759
essentially does nothing. Imagine you are standing there,

316
00:17:00,560 --> 00:17:07,600
you have not pressed any button, and

317
00:17:03,759 --> 00:17:09,520
obviously nothing should happen. This return FALSE

318
00:17:07,600 --> 00:17:12,959
does exactly that: do nothing.

319
00:17:09,520 --> 00:17:15,120
Now we say that in the next

320
00:17:12,959 --> 00:17:17,039
step,

321
00:17:15,120 --> 00:17:21,240
we enter this loop. This time we pressed the key.

322
00:17:17,039 --> 00:17:25,400
When we press the key,

323
00:17:21,240 --> 00:17:28,520
this pin value

324
00:17:25,400 --> 00:17:31,240
becomes zero. Its initial value

325
00:17:28,520 --> 00:17:34,000
was one, and when pressed it becomes

326
00:17:31,240 --> 00:17:37,720
zero. When it becomes zero, it puts zero into

327
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:40,120
reading. When reading becomes zero, this IF statement

328
00:17:37,720 --> 00:17:43,160
asks whether zero is different from last_button_state.

329
00:17:40,120 --> 00:17:46,400
last_button_state was

330
00:17:43,160 --> 00:17:48,919
one, so the values are different and we enter

331
00:17:46,400 --> 00:17:51,000
this loop.

332
00:17:48,919 --> 00:17:55,159
Here it says to put HAL_GetTick into last_debounce_time.

333
00:17:51,000 --> 00:18:00,559
What is HAL_GetTick? In the HAL functions

334
00:17:55,159 --> 00:18:03,600
we have a function called HAL_GetTick.

335
00:18:00,559 --> 00:18:07,240
HAL_GetTick, when the CPU runs

336
00:18:03,600 --> 00:18:10,840
the program, starts counting time

337
00:18:07,240 --> 00:18:14,760
from the beginning. It is not a counter, do not confuse it.

338
00:18:10,840 --> 00:18:17,000
It simply measures the elapsed time

339
00:18:14,760 --> 00:18:19,600
up to the current moment.

340
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:21,520
For example, suppose the program started,

341
00:18:19,600 --> 00:18:24,320
went down through the lines, and reached

342
00:18:21,520 --> 00:18:27,320
this point. Suppose 100 milliseconds have passed

343
00:18:24,320 --> 00:18:31,760
at this moment. That elapsed time is

344
00:18:27,320 --> 00:18:34,440
put into last_debounce_time,

345
00:18:31,760 --> 00:18:37,600
or, in other words, the time since the last debounce

346
00:18:34,440 --> 00:18:40,720
event. So last_debounce_time becomes 100.

347
00:18:37,600 --> 00:18:43,919
Then it enters this loop and says HAL_GetTick

348
00:18:40,720 --> 00:18:46,960
minus last_debounce_time; their difference,

349
00:18:43,919 --> 00:18:50,880
which is initially zero, so it does not enter this IF.

350
00:18:46,960 --> 00:18:52,880
In other words, it does not enter the main debounce loop

351
00:18:50,880 --> 00:18:55,720
until the 20-millisecond debounce time

352
00:18:52,880 --> 00:18:57,559
that we defined here has passed. So it keeps

353
00:18:55,720 --> 00:19:01,200
looping and checking again.

354
00:18:57,559 --> 00:19:03,559
The key is still being pressed,

355
00:19:01,200 --> 00:19:07,600
because you have not lifted your finger yet.

356
00:19:03,559 --> 00:19:10,559
So it remains in the same pressed state.

357
00:19:07,600 --> 00:19:12,919
Again reading is zero, because

358
00:19:10,559 --> 00:19:18,039
the button is still pressed, which is different from

359
00:19:12,919 --> 00:19:21,120
last_button_state. Then it updates the last debounce time.

360
00:19:18,039 --> 00:19:24,039
Now it passes this loop and comes here. Since

361
00:19:21,120 --> 00:19:26,200
the button is still being pressed, its state

362
00:19:24,039 --> 00:19:29,039
has not changed, so it does not enter this loop.

363
00:19:26,200 --> 00:19:31,640
It comes to this loop again and says last_debounce_time

364
00:19:29,039 --> 00:19:35,280
was 100. It has gone around one more time

365
00:19:31,640 --> 00:19:38,159
and HAL_GetTick is now, for example,

366
00:19:35,280 --> 00:19:41,520
105 or 110 milliseconds, and keeps increasing.

367
00:19:38,159 --> 00:19:44,679
It says that while you are pressing the key, time keeps

368
00:19:41,520 --> 00:19:46,679
moving forward until

369
00:19:44,679 --> 00:19:49,760
HAL_GetTick becomes 120 or higher and last

370
00:19:46,679 --> 00:19:54,600
debounce time is 100, so the difference becomes

371
00:19:49,760 --> 00:19:57,640
HAL_GetTick becomes 120 or higher, while last

372
00:19:54,600 --> 00:20:00,600
debounce time is 100, so the difference becomes

373
00:19:57,640 --> 00:20:02,840
20 or more, for example 25 or 30 milliseconds.

374
00:20:00,600 --> 00:20:06,000
That means the key you pressed has remained pressed

375
00:20:02,840 --> 00:20:08,679
for 25 or 30 milliseconds. Then it enters this loop.

376
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:12,679
Now it says reading is zero, while button_state

377
00:20:08,679 --> 00:20:16,000
is the button status, whose initial value

378
00:20:12,679 --> 00:20:19,000
was one. Are they different? Yes, so it enters

379
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:21,840
this loop. When it enters this loop,

380
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:25,400
it puts the zero reading into button_state.

381
00:20:21,840 --> 00:20:26,240
In other words, button_state, which was one, now becomes zero.

382
00:20:25,400 --> 00:20:28,919
Why does it do this? Because when you release

383
00:20:26,240 --> 00:20:30,840
So button_state, which was one, now becomes zero. Why?

384
00:20:28,919 --> 00:20:32,720
Because you want to release your finger again, and when

385
00:20:30,840 --> 00:20:36,120
you release it, at that moment the button_state

386
00:20:32,720 --> 00:20:39,840
should be zero, meaning that its state has changed.

387
00:20:36,120 --> 00:20:43,799
Now we say that button_state becomes zero here.

388
00:20:39,840 --> 00:20:46,360
Then we say that button_state is now zero.

389
00:20:43,799 --> 00:20:50,000
Since button_state is zero, it is equal to RESET.

390
00:20:46,360 --> 00:20:52,760
We say yes, so it enters this loop and

391
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:56,200
sets reading equal to last_button_state,

392
00:20:52,760 --> 00:20:59,720
which represents the last state of the button.

393
00:20:56,200 --> 00:21:02,360
Now look here: we have entered this loop.

394
00:20:59,720 --> 00:21:04,520
We defined a 20-millisecond debounce

395
00:21:02,360 --> 00:21:08,520
for the button so that it goes through these loops and

396
00:21:04,520 --> 00:21:11,480
returns TRUE.

397
00:21:08,520 --> 00:21:14,640
In other words, it returns one

398
00:21:11,480 --> 00:21:16,880
and goes to the while loop, where it can now

399
00:21:14,640 --> 00:21:19,440
increase something, or perform another action, such as

400
00:21:16,880 --> 00:21:22,760
turning an LED on or off, or do anything else.

401
00:21:19,440 --> 00:21:25,799
So we added debouncing for the push button and then

402
00:21:22,760 --> 00:21:30,000
returned TRUE so that it enters the main loop.

403
00:21:25,799 --> 00:21:32,520
Now I have written my while loop

404
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:36,919
so that time is not wasted.

405
00:21:32,520 --> 00:21:40,360
I placed it here like this.

406
00:21:36,919 --> 00:21:42,799
Here I write the code related to the while loop.

407
00:21:40,360 --> 00:21:46,640
It is written like this. Here it says

408
00:21:42,799 --> 00:21:50,360
if this function—although I should define this function

409
00:21:46,640 --> 00:21:53,919
higher up. In this section we get an error,

410
00:21:50,360 --> 00:21:57,320
and this is because we need to write the function

411
00:21:53,919 --> 00:22:01,400
in this section so that there is no problem.

412
00:21:57,320 --> 00:22:03,520
You should also know this from C programming:

413
00:22:01,400 --> 00:22:07,279
C programming uses function prototypes.

414
00:22:03,520 --> 00:22:11,240
We need to put this function prototype

415
00:22:07,279 --> 00:22:13,360
in this section.

416
00:22:11,240 --> 00:22:15,840
Here, after the PA0 definition, we place it.

417
00:22:13,360 --> 00:22:19,440
As I said, it has two inputs: one is the port,

418
00:22:15,840 --> 00:22:21,080
and the other is the pin of that port.

419
00:22:19,440 --> 00:22:25,880
Here we say that if button_debounce—that is,

420
00:22:21,080 --> 00:22:29,000
the same function we used earlier—is given GPIOA

421
00:22:25,880 --> 00:22:31,600
as the port and pin 0, meaning that we pressed a button,

422
00:22:29,000 --> 00:22:35,039
and as explained below, if its debounce has been completed,

423
00:22:31,600 --> 00:22:39,440
this line means that you pressed the button

424
00:22:35,039 --> 00:22:42,279
and the debounce has been completed.

425
00:22:39,440 --> 00:22:44,960
Then increase m by one. What was the initial value of m?

426
00:22:42,279 --> 00:22:47,679
It was zero. Now m becomes one. It says if n

427
00:22:44,960 --> 00:22:49,960
is 10, meaning we need ten increments so that m becomes zero.

428
00:22:47,679 --> 00:22:52,640
We have not reached that point yet, so one is added and m becomes one.

429
00:22:49,960 --> 00:22:54,679
Another point to remember in a C loop is that you can put

430
00:22:52,640 --> 00:22:55,440
braces here for n equal to zero and continue after this.

431
00:22:54,679 --> 00:22:58,360
But if you want the code to look cleaner, when there is only

432
00:22:55,440 --> 00:23:01,000
one statement, you do not need braces; this is enough.

433
00:22:58,360 --> 00:23:04,360
So we pressed the key and n changed from zero to one.

434
00:23:01,000 --> 00:23:07,400
Now what is this line? It says WritePin.

435
00:23:04,360 --> 00:23:09,600
You know what GPIO_WritePin is: it

436
00:23:07,400 --> 00:23:12,520
writes zero or one to the output pins.

437
00:23:09,600 --> 00:23:14,960
The meaning of WritePin is that it comes here and

438
00:23:12,520 --> 00:23:17,440
sets a group of pins on port A to zero or one.

439
00:23:14,960 --> 00:23:20,559
I will explain this part. These are the A pins

440
00:23:17,440 --> 00:23:23,039
that we said are from A1 to A7.

441
00:23:20,559 --> 00:23:25,799
It sets them.

442
00:23:23,039 --> 00:23:28,440
Set means ON, right? But in fact here

443
00:23:25,799 --> 00:23:30,640
we are turning the seven-segment display OFF.

444
00:23:28,440 --> 00:23:33,600
Why? Because our display is common-anode.

445
00:23:30,640 --> 00:23:36,039
Since it is common-anode, its cathode side

446
00:23:33,600 --> 00:23:38,799
must be connected to zero.

447
00:23:36,039 --> 00:23:41,039
So here we set the GPIO pins

448
00:23:38,799 --> 00:23:44,279
so that all the seven segments are OFF.

449
00:23:41,039 --> 00:23:46,400
Then it says to shift 0x7F to the left.

450
00:23:44,279 --> 00:23:49,120
Why does it do this? Remember, everyone,

451
00:23:46,400 --> 00:23:51,799
we said on the slides that PA0

452
00:23:49,120 --> 00:23:55,520
was configured as an input.

453
00:23:51,799 --> 00:23:58,159
We reserved it, so therefore we must not

454
00:23:55,520 --> 00:24:01,679
use it as an output anymore.

455
00:23:58,159 --> 00:24:03,480
So we shift one bit to the left

456
00:24:01,679 --> 00:24:06,880
so that PA1 through PA7

457
00:24:03,480 --> 00:24:10,559
are used for the numbers on the seven-segment display.

458
00:24:06,880 --> 00:24:14,360
Therefore, we shift it once to the left in this way,

459
00:24:10,559 --> 00:24:18,400
so that PA0 is reserved for the input

460
00:24:14,360 --> 00:24:22,919
and the rest are used as outputs.

461
00:24:18,400 --> 00:24:26,760
So this instruction sets all the A pins,

462
00:24:22,919 --> 00:24:29,880
or in other words, turns all the LEDs on the segment OFF,

463
00:24:26,760 --> 00:24:32,919
because it is common-anode.

464
00:24:29,880 --> 00:24:35,679
This line of code does the same thing.

465
00:24:32,919 --> 00:24:38,799
Again, WritePin is the function that turns a group of pins

466
00:24:35,679 --> 00:24:41,480
on port A—or any other port—ON or OFF.

467
00:24:38,799 --> 00:24:44,159
Here the port is A and it says to reset all of them.

468
00:24:41,480 --> 00:24:45,799
Again, because the display is common-anode,

469
00:24:44,159 --> 00:24:47,679
we use RESET here for the pins

470
00:24:45,799 --> 00:24:50,960
that should turn the corresponding LEDs ON.

471
00:24:47,679 --> 00:24:53,440
Now how does it turn them ON? Which pins should they be?

472
00:24:50,960 --> 00:24:55,520
Here we use the ten-element array

473
00:24:53,440 --> 00:25:00,440
that we defined earlier.

474
00:24:55,520 --> 00:25:05,559
It says that the value of m can be one, two, three,

475
00:25:00,440 --> 00:25:07,399
or anything else. Then shift it to the left again.

476
00:25:05,559 --> 00:25:10,559
Why again? For the same reason: PA0 is reserved for input,

477
00:25:07,399 --> 00:25:13,480
and PA1 through PA7 are outputs.

478
00:25:10,559 --> 00:25:19,520
The value of m is therefore sent to PA1 through PA7.

479
00:25:13,480 --> 00:25:24,279
m can be one, two, three, zero, or another value.

480
00:25:19,520 --> 00:25:28,720
That number is increased. At the first moment m is one,

481
00:25:24,279 --> 00:25:31,440
then it becomes two, then three, and so on.

482
00:25:28,720 --> 00:25:34,320
It increases one by one with each push-button press.

483
00:25:31,440 --> 00:25:37,120
So in the while loop, by pressing one key, we increase m

484
00:25:34,320 --> 00:25:40,159
so that with each press m increases once,

485
00:25:37,120 --> 00:25:42,600
and the corresponding value is displayed on the segment.

486
00:25:40,159 --> 00:25:44,200
This is the whole project. Now, before we

487
00:25:42,600 --> 00:25:47,240
build it, we come to this section, the compilation settings.

488
00:25:44,200 --> 00:25:51,320
Our debugger should be set to ST-Link. Be careful.

489
00:25:47,240 --> 00:25:55,640
Our target should also be one version higher; always remember this.

490
00:25:51,320 --> 00:25:59,320
Here we used P in CubeMX. In this section it should be 6

491
00:25:55,640 --> 00:26:02,480
so that we do not get an error here.

492
00:25:59,320 --> 00:26:05,640
We build it

493
00:26:02,480 --> 00:26:07,360
and wait for the build output

494
00:26:05,640 --> 00:26:10,559
to be shown, with no warnings and no errors.

495
00:26:07,360 --> 00:26:12,760
It takes a little time for the result to appear.

496
00:26:10,559 --> 00:26:16,559
Yes, here it completed without errors or warnings.

497
00:26:12,760 --> 00:26:20,760
Next, we come to this section and

498
00:26:16,559 --> 00:26:22,919
load the generated program.

499
00:26:20,760 --> 00:26:25,880
We click Load here.

500
00:26:22,919 --> 00:26:29,720
Yes, we have not connected the programmer yet.

501
00:26:25,880 --> 00:26:32,880
I am connecting the programmer here. Yes,

502
00:26:29,720 --> 00:26:36,320
the programmer is connected. Now we click Load.

503
00:26:32,880 --> 00:26:39,840
Yes, the program has been loaded onto our board.

504
00:26:36,320 --> 00:26:42,279
All right, everyone, we programmed the program.

505
00:26:39,840 --> 00:26:45,080
We press the Reset button once.

506
00:26:42,279 --> 00:26:50,600
And here is our push button. As you can see,

507
00:26:45,080 --> 00:26:54,159
we configured PA0 as an input.

508
00:26:50,600 --> 00:26:57,320
We connected this side to the push button.

509
00:26:54,159 --> 00:27:00,760
It may not be very clear here, but we connected it

510
00:26:57,320 --> 00:27:05,399
to the push button and connected the other side

511
00:27:00,760 --> 00:27:07,679
to ground. This cable is the ground connection.

512
00:27:05,399 --> 00:27:12,159
We also connected PA1 through PA7

513
00:27:07,679 --> 00:27:14,440
to the seven-segment display. Here I said to use

514
00:27:12,159 --> 00:27:15,960
a 220-ohm resistor.

515
00:27:14,440 --> 00:27:18,159
Its color code is red, red, brown. You should connect it

516
00:27:15,960 --> 00:27:21,039
so that your LED does not burn out.

517
00:27:18,159 --> 00:27:23,919
Now I press the push button once.

518
00:27:21,039 --> 00:27:26,679
As you can see, with each press only one number

519
00:27:23,919 --> 00:27:29,760
increases, and when it reaches 9 it

520
00:27:26,679 --> 00:27:35,440
returns to zero again.

521
00:27:29,760 --> 00:27:39,360
We build it and wait until

522
00:27:35,440 --> 00:27:43,159
the build output is shown,

523
00:27:39,360 --> 00:27:49,200
with no warnings and no errors. It takes a little time

524
00:27:43,159 --> 00:27:49,200
for the result to appear.

525
00:28:00,000 --> 00:28:08,200
